# Autonomous development

{{PROJECT}} is developed by **one orchestrator session** in Bypass mode, in `{{PROJECT_DIR}}`, with **sub-agents**
(Claude Code's own Agent tool) doing the work in git worktrees, without a person in the loop. A person approves
deploys to protected environments and answers the required escalations; nothing else waits for one.

Read first: `CLAUDE.md`, `CHARTER.md` (binds every agent), `DECISIONS.md` (never act against a row) and
`orchestrator.md` (the orchestrator's brief). Everything below binds the orchestrator AND every sub-agent.

## Ground rules

1. **No questions.** Nobody answers in real time. Take the PRD's recommended default, write the decision on the
   ticket and in the status file, go on. Do not use AskUserQuestion. Only a required escalation (a credential is
   one) goes under NEEDS A HUMAN in the status file; move to the next item. A check you cannot do here (a real
   device) is written on its ticket as not verified, not handed to the owner.
2. **Worktrees.** A sub-agent works in its own git worktree, never in `{{PROJECT_DIR}}` and never in another
   agent's worktree: `git worktree add {{PROJECT_DIR}}.worktrees/<name> -b ticket/T-<n>-<slug> origin/{{INTEGRATION_BRANCH}}`.
   The Agent tool's `isolation: "worktree"` also works for a short task that needs no installed dependencies.
3. **Safety, whatever Bypass lets you do.**
   - Never print, log, commit or paste a credential. No token-shaped literal anywhere in the repository.
   - Do not print the environment, read credential files or dump cluster Secrets.
   - `kubectl`: read freely. Change things only in a rig namespace you made with `scripts/dev-rig.sh`, and delete
     it when you are done. Never touch the application's, the workspaces' or the GitOps namespaces.
   - Never push `{{DEFAULT_BRANCH}}`. Never approve a deploy or work around its approval.
   - Never force push, rewrite pushed history, delete a remote branch, use `--no-verify` or amend a pushed commit.
   - `.claude/settings.json` denies these even in Bypass. That is deliberate: do not look for another way (a script,
     `sh -c`, another tool). Write it under NEEDS A HUMAN instead.
4. **Heavy jobs leave the workspace.** Full suites, typechecks, production builds, browsers and timing runs go
   through `scripts/job-run.sh -- '<command>'`, a cluster Job on another node (push the branch first:
   `git push origin HEAD:wip/<name>`). At most 3 Jobs at once and 2 per chat. Only what must run in the workspace
   (installing a worktree's dependencies, a local preview) runs there, one at a time through the shared lock:
   `flock /tmp/heavy.lock nice -n 10 <command>`. Until `scripts/job-run.sh` exists, EVERY heavy job runs that way.
   A single test file may run directly. Keep `/tmp` clean.
5. **WIP limit five.** At most five sub-agents run at once, fewer when the quota projection is high.
6. **Do not loop.** The same command failing three times means: change the approach, or move to the next item and
   write down why.
7. **Quota.** The portal moves a chat to another Claude when one runs out. On a limit error, wait a few minutes and
   go on.
8. **No SendMessage to the main conversation.** A sub-agent reports in its final message, its commits, files and
   tickets only, and every brief says so. Chat to chat goes through the Portal MCP `session_message`.
9. **No other Bypass sessions.** Sub-agents of the Agent tool are the way to run work in parallel.

## Who integrates

Sub-agents commit on their own branch and push it (`git push -u origin <branch>`). Every commit ends with its
trailers as their own paragraph:

```text
Ticket: T-<n>
Pilot-Session: <the orchestrator's chat session id>
Pilot-Agent: main | <the sub-agent's label>
```

When a commit hook writes these, a sub-agent exports `PILOT_AGENT=<label>` instead. **Only the orchestrator
pushes `{{INTEGRATION_BRANCH}}`:**

```sh
cd {{PROJECT_DIR}} && git fetch origin && git merge --no-ff origin/<branch>   # never discard somebody's lines
{{TEST_QUICK}}                                                                  # the fast lane
git push origin HEAD:{{INTEGRATION_BRANCH}} && git push pilot HEAD:{{INTEGRATION_BRANCH}}   # if refused: fetch, merge, gate again
```

Then the sub-agent on that branch brings it up to date: rebase ONLY commits that were never pushed; once a branch is
pushed, merge `origin/{{INTEGRATION_BRANCH}}` into it instead, because a rebased pushed branch could only go back with
a force push, which is denied.

## Two lanes

- **Integration, the fast lane.** `{{TEST_QUICK}}` (minutes: the checks for what changed), then push. After every
  slice, and at least every 8 commits or every 2 hours: nothing piles up in a workspace.
- **Release, the full lane.** `{{TEST_FULL}}` on the exact commit before any deploy (the deploy pipeline's `gate`
  input stays true; `false` only on the owner's explicit word for that run), and every two hours on the head of `{{INTEGRATION_BRANCH}}`, as a cluster Job
  (`scripts/gate-job.sh <commit>`). A red full gate on `{{INTEGRATION_BRANCH}}` is fixed first, before any new
  slice is merged: `{{INTEGRATION_BRANCH}}` stays shippable.

Sub-agents run only their own package's checks and test files, never a full gate or a build.

## Migrations

If the project has database migrations: append only, never edit a released one, and give each parallel slice its
own block of numbers (written in the status file) so two slices never take the same number.

## Status and final report

`docs/dev-sessions/status/orchestrator.md`, at most 80 lines, overwritten, committed with the work, in the shape of
`status/_template.md`: DONE, IN FLIGHT, DECISIONS, NEEDS A HUMAN, NEXT. After a restart, the orchestrator and the
Keep going prompt read it first. When the backlog is empty: make `TRACK COMPLETE` its first line, commit and push
it, and end with a final report of at most 25 lines.

## Keeping it going

The portal's **Keep going** policy on the orchestrator chat nudges it when idle, wakes it when interrupted and
starts it again from the status file when it ends (with caps), and leaves it alone once the status file starts with
`TRACK COMPLETE` or a person closed the chat. It never answers a tool approval and never approves a deploy. Bypass
survives a restart only under a time-boxed lease the owner grants. Where Keep going is not available, a monitor
runs **outside** the workspace: a credential that can steer chats is never kept where agents can read it.
